@anon.a2b023d1a4e2834952d16152dce23780.1162 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [1306 x i8] }> <{ [1306 x i8] c"A sequence of Unicode codepoints.\0A\0AYou can iterate over the grapheme clusters of the string using a [for\0Aloop]($scripting/#loops). Grapheme clusters are basically characters but\0Akeep together things that belong together, e.g. multiple codepoints that\0Atogether form a flag emoji. Strings can be added with the `+` operator,\0A[joined together]($scripting/#blocks) and multiplied with integers.\0A\0ATypst provides utility methods for string manipulation. Many of these\0Amethods (e.g., `split`, `trim` and `replace`) operate on _patterns:_ A\0Apattern can be either a string or a [regular expression]($regex). This makes\0Athe methods quite versatile.\0A\0AAll lengths and indices are expressed in terms of UTF-8 bytes. Indices are\0Azero-based and negative indices wrap around to the end of the string.\0A\0AYou can convert a value to a string with this type's constructor.\0A\0A# Example\0A```example\0A#\22hello world!\22 \\\0A#\22\\\22hello\\n  world\\\22!\22 \\\0A#\221 2 3\22.split() \\\0A#\221,2;3\22.split(regex(\22[,;]\22)) \\\0A#(regex(\22\\d+\22) in \22ten euros\22) \\\0A#(regex(\22\\d+\22) in \2210 euros\22)\0A```\0A\0A# Escape sequences { #escapes }\0AJust like in markup, you can escape a few symbols in strings:\0A- `[\\\\]` for a backslash\0A- `[\\\22]` for a quote\0A- `[\\n]` for a newline\0A- `[\\r]` for a carriage return\0A- `[\\t]` for a tab\0A- `[\\u{1f600}]` for a hexadecimal Unicode escape sequence" }>, align 1
